            <ArticleTitle>Upper gastrointestinal complaints as a consequence of thoracic spinal tumor.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>388-91</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A rare case of a 43-year-old male with chronic epigastric complaints and atypical diffuse osseous pain for two years, which were finally found to be caused by a benign thoracic spinal tumor (lipoma) and was successfully treated by neurosurgical resection, is presented. At three years follow-up postoperatively he remains completely asymptomatic. This report discusses the case and the potential pathophysiology of the patient's symptoms.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
